What counts as content
----------------------

- every topic page: summary, body paragraphs, milestones, concepts, people and
  practice tasks
- the hardware course pages: their explanations, figures and captions
- the about page, the domain and path introductions, the course notes
- the social card image (og.png)

What is code, and therefore MIT instead
--------------------------------------

- the site shell (index.html), stylesheets, page modules and data files as
  software
- the build, check and test scripts under dev/

You can tell the two apart in practice: if you want to reuse the *text* of a
topic, CC BY-SA applies; if you want to reuse the *engine* that renders it, MIT
applies.

If you reuse the content
------------------------

- give credit: name the atlas ("CS Atlas") and link back to the page you used
- say what you changed, if you changed it
- keep the same licence: anything derived from this content must also be
  CC BY-SA 4.0

Short quotes with a link back do not need to follow the share-alike clause; it
applies when you republish substantial portions.
